+### Compound Durations
+
+Use `parseCompound` to sum several parts into milliseconds. Parts can be separated by spaces, a comma, or nothing. Every part needs a unit; invalid input throws a `TypeError`.
+
+```js
+import { parseCompound } from 'ms';
+
+parseCompound('1h 30m'); // 5400000
+parseCompound('1h, 30m'); // 5400000
+parseCompound('2 days 4 hours'); // 187200000
+parseCompound('1h30m15s'); // 5415000
+```
+
+### Clock Strings
+
+Use `parseClock` for timer-style strings: `m:ss` and `h:mm:ss`. Unlike `parseCompound`, it returns `undefined` on invalid input instead of throwing, so TypeScript makes you handle the failure case.
+
+```js
+import { parseClock } from 'ms';
+
+parseClock('1:30'); // 90000
+parseClock('01:02:03'); // 3723000
+parseClock('0:01.5'); // 1500
+parseClock('1:60'); // undefined (seconds must be 00-59)
+parseClock('abc'); // undefined
+```
+
 ### Enhanced Type Safety
 
 If you want strict type checking for the input value, you can use `parseStrict`.
